WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.660
A birthday predicts a layer.

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.660
But is the birthday the cause, or only a record?

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:10.093
Two explanations are tangled.

4
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:12.933
The progenitor might carry its own schedule.

5
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:17.367
Or the surroundings at the time of birth might tell its daughter where to go.

6
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:24.000
In 1991, Susan McConnell and Christine Kaznowski set the two calendars against each

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.100
other, in ferrets.

8
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.230
Why ferrets? Their visual cortex keeps making neurons after birth.

9
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:34.533
So a newborn kit is a host cortex at the upper-layer stage.

10
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:37.377
They ran the experiment in four steps.

11
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.840
First, pulse a donor embryo with tritiated thymidine, at a stage that makes

12
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.840
deep-layer neurons.

13
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.540
Only cells in S phase take it up.

14
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:48.593
Second, harvest the cells.

15
00:00:49.493 --> 00:00:53.253
Take them out soon after the pulse, or later, in G2, near division.

16
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:58.913
Third, graft them into a newborn kit's cortex, which is making layers 2 and 3.

17
00:00:59.693 --> 00:01:01.633
Fourth, find the labeled neurons later.

18
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:03.903
The harvest is the key step.

19
00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:07.943
A cell grafted in S phase finishes its cycle in the host.

20
00:01:08.723 --> 00:01:10.003
So its daughter is born there.

21
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:15.137
Predict. Where do the daughters settle if the cells were moved in S phase?

22
00:01:16.057 --> 00:01:17.797
And if they were moved late in the cycle?

23
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.230
Cells moved in S phase mostly settled in layers 2 and 3, like the host's own

24
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.230
neurons.

25
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:31.973
They finished their final cycle in the older host.

26
00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:35.033
Its cues could act on them before they divided.

27
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:40.543
Cells moved late in the cycle mostly settled in the deep layers, out of step with

28
00:01:40.543 --> 00:01:43.883
the host. Their laminar fate was fixed before the move.

29
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:48.667
So the grafts bracket commitment.

30
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:54.447
Laminar fate still responds to the host in S phase, and is fixed by a later stage,

31
00:01:54.707 --> 00:01:57.807
before division. The exact moment is not known.

32
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:01.567
So the host's cues count as part of the progenitor's state.

33
00:02:02.347 --> 00:02:04.547
It is not only a clock sealed inside the cell.

34
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:06.937
Could an old progenitor go back?

35
00:02:07.777 --> 00:02:08.997
Run the graft the other way.

36
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:14.917
Predict. Late progenitors go into a young host, which offers deep-layer cues.

37
00:02:15.957 --> 00:02:16.957
What do they make?

38
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.920
In 1996, Gretchen Frantz and McConnell did this.

39
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.140
The late progenitors still made upper-layer neurons.

40
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:32.153
They had lost deep-layer fates from their menu.

41
00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:34.813
So they could not answer the young host's cues.

42
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:41.357
In these ferret experiments, competence narrowed one way.

43
00:02:42.097 --> 00:02:47.497
But a 2019 mouse study found that some progenitors moved into a younger host can

44
00:02:47.497 --> 00:02:48.817
return to earlier states.

45
00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.807
The takeaway: the birthday is a record.

46
00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:53.967
Schedule or surroundings?

47
00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:59.727
Both. Host cues count until laminar fate is fixed late in the cycle.

48
00:03:00.607 --> 00:03:02.127
Then the menu usually shortens.
